00:11:36.278 --> 00:11:41.510
-[William] One October, a razor blade shows up in a little white girl's Halloween candy.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:42.506
-[William] Police come around here.

00:11:42.528 --> 00:11:43.206
-[William] Looking for Sherman.

00:11:43.238 --> 00:11:44.378
-[William] But Sherman's gone.

00:11:44.464 --> 00:11:47.546
Sherman must have known that this was not a good look for him and began

00:11:47.578 --> 00:11:50.318
hiding out in the walls of the apartment complex, where he became a bit of

00:11:50.324 --> 00:11:52.880
an urban legend himself among the neighborhood kids.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:55.001
-[Kid] I hope Sherman gets you!

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:58.414
And one October, morning, he emerges from a hole in the wall of the laundry room

00:11:58.462 --> 00:12:01.794
and offers Candy to a boy named William, which I'm not saying he

00:12:01.832 --> 00:12:05.058
deserved what happened to him, but he has to realize that coming out of a wall

00:12:05.064 --> 00:12:09.286
and offering candy in the area known as Candyman country is kind of creepy.

00:12:09.388 --> 00:12:13.074
Due to the stories, young William seems to agree that it's creepy

00:12:13.122 --> 00:12:13.986
and he screams.

00:12:14.018 --> 00:12:16.578
And Sherman is caught and beaten to death by police.

00:12:16.674 --> 00:12:20.406
But like Robitaille and presumably the others before him, he was innocent and

00:12:20.428 --> 00:12:22.822
his soul is intertwined in the Candyman folklore.

00:12:22.886 --> 00:12:25.766
His name disappeared from the stories over the years and he was simply known

00:12:25.798 --> 00:12:26.630
as Candyman.

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:30.186
And when William' sister eventually calls Candyman's name, he was the one to

00:12:30.208 --> 00:12:31.162
take revenge on her.

00:12:31.216 --> 00:12:34.314
It may seem like it's getting kind of confusing since we now have four

00:12:34.352 --> 00:12:36.154
different people who are all known as Candyman.

00:12:36.202 --> 00:12:39.022
But if you think about it, this tends to be how real urban legends go.

00:12:39.076 --> 00:12:42.266
As the story is passed from generation to generation, details get changed

00:12:42.298 --> 00:12:44.430
and bias from each time period creeps in.

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.682
It's not that different from the legend of Bloody Mary.

00:12:46.746 --> 00:12:47.714
Depending on who you ask today,

00:12:47.752 --> 00:12:50.690
People might tell you that Bloody Mary is one of a number of people,

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.366
including Queen Mary I of England, an American serial killer named Mary Worth,

00:12:54.398 --> 00:12:58.126
Hungarian serial killer Countess Elizabeth Bathory, or even

00:12:58.158 --> 00:13:00.666
a Salem witch trial victim named Mary from the early modern era.

00:13:00.718 --> 00:13:03.654
I honestly don't know how Elizabeth Bathory's name got in the mix because

00:13:03.692 --> 00:13:05.074
her name isn't even Mary.

00:13:05.122 --> 00:13:08.066
It might just be one of those things where two different urban legends merge

00:13:08.098 --> 00:13:11.650
with the passage of time, much like Sherman Fields and Daniel Robitaille.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.086
Also, quick timeline note about Sherman Fields.

00:13:14.118 --> 00:13:17.558
At one point there was an article claiming that he died in 1970,

00:13:17.574 --> 00:13:20.250
but I'd have to trust the title card that claims it was 1977.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.182
The title card is always all knowing.

00:13:22.246 --> 00:13:25.158
And to back this up, there's a flyer at an art gallery that claims that it

00:13:25.184 --> 00:13:26.430
happened in the late 70s.

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:29.582
This may be a continuity error, but I choose to look at it as another

00:13:29.636 --> 00:13:31.322
example of the nature of folklore.

00:13:31.386 --> 00:13:34.574
As retellings continually reshape a story, you end up with different

00:13:34.612 --> 00:13:36.282
versions that have different details.

00:13:36.346 --> 00:13:38.930
We see the same effect happen all the time in the news today.

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:41.858
I recently watched a video from a creator named Upper Echelon where he

00:13:41.864 --> 00:13:44.574
compares the modern news cycle to the children's game: Telephone.

00:13:44.622 --> 00:13:47.714
In telephone, you start with one phrase and each kid whispers it to the kid

00:13:47.752 --> 00:13:48.274
next to them.

00:13:48.312 --> 00:13:51.142
And once you get all the way around the circle, the original phrase is often

00:13:51.196 --> 00:13:52.130
changed entirely.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.254
And in his video, he talks about how the same thing is happening with news

00:13:55.292 --> 00:13:58.166
publishers writing articles on other news publishers articles, and then

00:13:58.188 --> 00:14:01.630
journalists getting their info secondhand from those articles, et cetera, et cetera.

00:14:01.650 --> 00:14:05.254
And you end up with the original statement being completely changed

00:14:05.302 --> 00:14:08.778
or exaggerated due to the bias in how each writer interprets the message of

00:14:08.784 --> 00:14:09.782
the previous writer.

00:14:09.846 --> 00:14:12.182
So the same thing seems to be going on in Candyman.

00:14:12.246 --> 00:14:15.886
In 1981, Philip Purcell, an anthropology professor at University of

00:14:15.908 --> 00:14:19.534
Illinois, Chicago, or UIC for short, wrote a paper on Candy man, which

00:14:19.572 --> 00:14:21.322
didn't exactly help the legend die.

00:14:21.386 --> 00:14:24.494
At some points, most likely in the mid-80s, a gang leader started

00:14:24.532 --> 00:14:26.970
terrorizing the neighborhood, calling himself Candyman.

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:29.826
While we don't know exactly what his motivation was, I imagine that he

00:14:29.848 --> 00:14:32.498
thought some non skeptics would come to believe that the angry spirit of

00:14:32.504 --> 00:14:35.550
Daniel Robitaille was the one committing his heinous crimes.

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:39.266
And that's essentially what happened on the particularly disturbing

00:14:39.298 --> 00:14:41.190
April 22, 1987.

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:46.098
-[Nurse] All I know was there was some lady in that tub and she heard a noise.

00:14:46.194 --> 00:14:48.338
-[Nurse] I think her name was Ruthie Jean.

00:14:48.434 --> 00:14:51.506
When Ruthie Jean called the police, they didn't believe her, giving ample

00:14:51.538 --> 00:14:54.166
time for the gangster to break into her apartment through the medicine

00:14:54.198 --> 00:14:57.030
cabinet that conjoined her bathroom to the vacant unit next to hers.

00:14:57.110 --> 00:14:59.302
She was later found to have been killed with a hook.

00:14:59.366 --> 00:15:03.082
And if that idea isn't disquieting enough, it may not bring you any comfort

00:15:03.146 --> 00:15:05.680
to learn that this actually happened.

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:08.392
(IMPACT)

00:15:10.928 --> 00:15:14.075
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:18.866
Ruthie May McCoy was a real woman who was killed in Chicago after a man came

00:15:18.888 --> 00:15:20.174
in through her medicine cabinet.

00:15:20.222 --> 00:15:23.266
And she was the inspiration for the identical crime that took place in the

00:15:23.288 --> 00:15:25.550
original 1992 film Candyman.

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.350
Ruthie McCoy's name was kind of split into two characters.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:31.586
Ruthie Jean, the woman who was killed in her apartment, and Anne Marie McCoy,

00:15:31.618 --> 00:15:34.946
her neighbor, who later faces some horrors of her own at the expense

00:15:34.978 --> 00:15:36.038
of her infant son.

00:15:36.124 --> 00:15:38.322
Ruthie May McCoy also had schizophrenia.

00:15:38.386 --> 00:15:41.206
In Candyman, the protagonist, Helen, is made to look like she has

00:15:41.228 --> 00:15:44.790
schizophrenia by Candyman to hide his involvement and make her look guilty.

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:47.894
This creates a direct link between the mental illness and the Candyman mythos.

00:15:47.942 --> 00:15:51.114
In real life, police didn't believe Ruthie when she called 911

00:15:51.152 --> 00:15:52.774
because they knew she had schizophrenia.

00:15:52.822 --> 00:15:55.214
But in the movie, they didn't believe her because they didn't believe

00:15:55.252 --> 00:15:56.058
in Candyman.

00:15:56.154 --> 00:15:59.434
A reporter for the Chicago Reader discovered that bathroom cabinet break-ins

00:15:59.482 --> 00:16:02.746
were quite common and well known by residents of the Abbot Homes apartments

00:16:02.778 --> 00:16:03.754
where McCoy lived.

00:16:03.802 --> 00:16:06.846
Apparently, the bathroom cabinets had been installed back to back with a two

00:16:06.868 --> 00:16:09.358
foot wide pipe chase running between the apartments.

00:16:09.454 --> 00:16:12.498
They were loosely attached to the walls with just six flimsy nails to make

00:16:12.504 --> 00:16:14.910
it easy for plumbers to access the pipes for repairs.

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:18.898
The newspaper in Candyman tells an almost identical story with, of course, a

00:16:18.904 --> 00:16:20.302
few key details changed.

00:16:20.366 --> 00:16:23.158
The killer or killers of Ruthie Jean smashed their way through the back of

00:16:23.164 --> 00:16:25.202
the medicine cabinet in the small apartment bathroom.

00:16:25.266 --> 00:16:27.766
The attack took place at the Cabrini-Green Housing Project.

00:16:27.868 --> 00:16:31.026
This mysterious attack brings the total of unsolved murders in the housing

00:16:31.058 --> 00:16:33.578
project to an alarming 26 just this year.

00:16:33.664 --> 00:16:37.274
It's interesting how the headline says what killed Ruthie Jean and not who killed Ruthie Jean?

00:16:37.312 --> 00:16:40.938
While the gangster posing as Candyman built fear in the

00:16:40.944 --> 00:16:44.206
community without summoning the real thing, the legend was also alive and

00:16:44.228 --> 00:16:45.758
well outside of Cabrini-Green.

00:16:45.844 --> 00:16:50.014
One particularly chilling example happened in the late 80s, maybe 1988.

00:16:50.132 --> 00:16:52.751
-[Monica] Happened a few years ago near Moses Lake in Indiana.

00:16:52.751 --> 00:16:55.038
A babysitter named Clara is visited by a guy she

00:16:55.044 --> 00:16:58.142
has the hots for named Billy and challenges him to try summoning Candyman

00:16:58.206 --> 00:17:00.450
but stops him short of saying his name the fifth time.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:06.000
After he goes downstairs she, for unknown reasons, completes the ritual by reciting the name for the fifth time.

00:17:06.194 --> 00:17:07.089
-[Clara] Candyman. 

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:09.862
When she turned off the lights she saw him standing there behind her.

00:17:09.916 --> 00:17:13.270
Her blood leaked through the ceiling and Candyman killed the baby too.

00:17:13.340 --> 00:17:15.938
Billy survived, but his hair turned white from shock.

00:17:16.034 --> 00:17:19.570
This brings up a few questions about the rules around summoning Candyman.

00:17:19.650 --> 00:17:21.654
In this instance, we learn a few things.

00:17:21.772 --> 00:17:25.098
Number 1- it doesn't have to be the same person saying the name each time.

00:17:25.184 --> 00:17:28.298
Whoever says it the fifth and final time will be considered the summoner and

00:17:28.304 --> 00:17:30.038
that'll be the person who faces his wrath.

00:17:30.134 --> 00:17:32.294
Number 2- there doesn't appear to be a time limit.

00:17:32.342 --> 00:17:35.678
You can pause as long as you want between each Candyman which people love to do.

00:17:35.684 --> 00:17:38.606
There's always a ridiculous dramatic pause before saying it for the

00:17:38.628 --> 00:17:39.230
final time.

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.806
In fact, the five declarations of the name don't even have to be consecutive.

00:17:41.828 --> 00:17:45.166
You can interrupt the ritual with other dialogue but the say my

00:17:45.188 --> 00:17:46.558
name counter doesn't stop.

00:17:46.644 --> 00:17:47.374
Around this time,

00:17:47.412 --> 00:17:50.306
There was also another story involving a handicapped boy who was killed by

00:17:50.328 --> 00:17:53.486
Candyman in a public restroom in Cabrini-Green while his mom was shopping

00:17:53.518 --> 00:17:54.302
across the street.

00:17:54.366 --> 00:17:57.858
When a man went in to check on him it said that his hair also turned white.

00:17:57.944 --> 00:18:01.206
This boy was most likely a victim of the gangster posing as Candyman, not

00:18:01.228 --> 00:18:03.042
the supernatural Candyman himself.

00:18:03.186 --> 00:18:07.154
Then there's the most notable victim, a graduate student at UIC named Helen Lyle.

00:18:07.202 --> 00:18:10.774
-[Purcell] Cabrini Green, Chicago, 1992.

00:18:10.892 --> 00:18:14.666
Yeah, I'm going to ignore this newspaper headline from 1991 and assume that

00:18:14.688 --> 00:18:17.178
the guy who wrote a book on Candyman has his dates correct.

00:18:17.264 --> 00:18:18.570
1992 it is.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:21.606
Helen and her classmate Bernadette are working on a project on urban legends.

00:18:21.638 --> 00:18:24.998
She's also married to a professor, Trevor who teaches a class on

00:18:25.024 --> 00:18:25.882
modern folklore.

00:18:25.946 --> 00:18:28.718
On the board you can see some of the common elements that he's identified in

00:18:28.724 --> 00:18:29.690
neighborhood legends.

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:31.754
Some, if only a little, basis in truth.

00:18:31.802 --> 00:18:35.678
Often a victim seeking revenge, resides in a forbidden area,

00:18:35.684 --> 00:18:36.818
sometimes to keep children from the area.

00:18:36.904 --> 00:18:39.714
This is also probably a reference to the original short story and

00:18:39.752 --> 00:18:42.734
deformities and appliances, blades, hooks, etc.

00:18:42.862 --> 00:18:45.646
These are obviously all traits that apply to the Candyman legend.

00:18:45.678 --> 00:18:48.894
But he sees these urban legends as more of a reflection of societal fears

00:18:48.942 --> 00:18:52.406
rather than a supernatural phenomenon which is basically what Candyman is in

00:18:52.428 --> 00:18:52.928
our world.

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:54.838
We have a term for that here in Horror History.

00:18:54.924 --> 00:18:56.066
 Cultural Legendizing.

00:18:56.098 --> 00:18:59.574
Candyman is a metaphor for racial segregation in the same way that Godzilla

00:18:59.622 --> 00:19:02.902
represents nuclear fallout, the Demogorgon represents fears of communism,

00:19:02.966 --> 00:19:06.490
and La Llorona represents issues in Central American marriage practices.

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:11.510
-[Trevor] See, these stories are modern, oral folklore.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:15.674
-[Trevor] They are the unselfconscious reflection of the fears of urban society.

00:19:15.802 --> 00:19:18.766
As part of her research, Helen talks to a woman familiar with the story of

00:19:18.788 --> 00:19:19.306
Ruthie Jean,

00:19:19.338 --> 00:19:22.250
And when Candyman is brought up, she becomes visibly uncomfortable.

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:25.326
She eventually discovers that her own apartment was originally supposed to

00:19:25.348 --> 00:19:28.450
be a housing project with the same layout as the building in Cabrini-Green.

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.794
And after replacing her mirror, her and Bernadette recite the illicit name

00:19:31.832 --> 00:19:32.802
into it four times.

00:19:32.856 --> 00:19:36.706
But notably, only Helen completes the ritual by saying the fifth after a

00:19:36.728 --> 00:19:38.322
four second pause for dramatic effect of course.

00:19:38.376 --> 00:19:41.682
However, Candyman does not act against Helen immediately.

00:19:41.746 --> 00:19:44.738
The next day, they go to visit the actual apartment where Ruthie Jean lost

00:19:44.754 --> 00:19:47.718
her life in Cabrini-Green, and they're instantly spotted and suspected to be

00:19:47.724 --> 00:19:48.706
policed by the locals.

00:19:48.738 --> 00:19:51.714
They discover graffiti referencing the original legend and a huge painting

00:19:51.762 --> 00:19:52.386
of Robitaille

00:19:52.418 --> 00:19:55.658
In what's left of Ruthie Jean's former apartment, she also finds a pile of

00:19:55.664 --> 00:19:57.622
candy, one of which contains a razor blade.

00:19:57.686 --> 00:20:00.586
Could this mean that someone in the building is the actual criminal who

00:20:00.608 --> 00:20:03.178
framed Sherman Fields? I think that may be the case.

00:20:03.264 --> 00:20:07.198
Fields passed away in 1977, and Ruthie Jean's apartment wouldn't have been

00:20:07.204 --> 00:20:08.798
vacated until ten years later.

00:20:08.884 --> 00:20:11.738
Meaning that this candy was placed here well after his death.

00:20:11.834 --> 00:20:13.834
This is where Candyman first approaches Helen.

00:20:13.882 --> 00:20:15.662
However, she doesn't realize it until later when reviewing the photos.

00:20:15.716 --> 00:20:18.846
On the way out, they meet a woman named Annemarie McCoy,

00:20:18.878 --> 00:20:21.698
who explains that she heard Ruthie screaming through the wall the

00:20:21.704 --> 00:20:23.394
night of the murder and dialed 911.

00:20:23.432 --> 00:20:24.242
But nobody came.

00:20:24.296 --> 00:20:27.106
Annemarie claims that she's scared for her son because they're never going

00:20:27.128 --> 00:20:28.158
to catch Candyman.

00:20:28.254 --> 00:20:32.006
Helen would later go back alone and meet a boy named Jake, who refuses to

00:20:32.028 --> 00:20:34.070
talk out of fear that the Candyman will get him.

00:20:34.140 --> 00:20:37.206
He does, however, lead her to the bathroom where the other young boy was

00:20:37.228 --> 00:20:37.794
once killed.

00:20:37.842 --> 00:20:40.966
She finds more sweets to the sweet graffiti and all of the toilets are

00:20:40.988 --> 00:20:44.566
destroyed except for the last one, which is completely filled with bees.

00:20:44.678 --> 00:20:47.398
She doesn't know it yet, but this is the first hallucination that she's

00:20:47.414 --> 00:20:49.510
seeing as a result of Candyman's influence.

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:52.042
Actually, I'm not sure if hallucination is the right word.

00:20:52.096 --> 00:20:55.038
When someone summons the Candyman, he or she can see the spirit and the

00:20:55.044 --> 00:20:57.514
colony of bees that come with it, but others cannot.

00:20:57.642 --> 00:20:59.450
However, they're not hallucinations.

00:20:59.530 --> 00:21:02.970
Candyman's hook really can pierce and the bees really can sting.

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:06.638
One thing that is definitely not a hallucination or spirit of any kind is

00:21:06.644 --> 00:21:09.342
the gang leader who's been going around calling himself Candyman.

00:21:09.406 --> 00:21:12.526
And when he shows up and traps her in the bathroom, it's bad news for Helen Lyle.

00:21:12.558 --> 00:21:14.562
She's lucky to survive his attack.

00:21:14.616 --> 00:21:17.666
But one silver lining is that the police are finally able to arrest the man

00:21:17.688 --> 00:21:18.782
who killed Ruthie Jean.

00:21:18.846 --> 00:21:21.270
Nobody was willing to testify against him until now.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:24.886
Once he's arrested, belief in the Candyman around Cabrini-Green reaches an

00:21:24.908 --> 00:21:28.214
all time low which prompts him to finally make his presence known to Helen

00:21:28.262 --> 00:21:31.258
as she's reviewing photos in the university parking lot.

00:21:31.424 --> 00:21:32.717
-[Helen] Do I know you?

00:21:32.717 --> 00:21:35.455
-[Candyman] No. But you doubted me.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:38.000
(IMPACT)

00:21:41.016 --> 00:21:44.601
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:49.034
Once Helen realizes who he really is, Candyman explains that he was obliged

00:21:49.082 --> 00:21:51.290
to come because she didn't believe in the legend.

00:21:51.450 --> 00:21:57.390
-[Candyman] I am the writing on the wall, the whisper in the classroom.

00:21:57.810 --> 00:22:01.120
-[Candyman] Without these things, I am nothing.

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:05.362
-[Candyman] So now I must shed innocent blood.

00:22:05.546 --> 00:22:08.646
Essentially, he's saying that he must continue to kill in order to keep his

00:22:08.668 --> 00:22:09.394
legend alive.

00:22:09.442 --> 00:22:12.726
But contrary to what you would expect based on his behavior so far,

00:22:12.748 --> 00:22:13.554
He doesn't kill Helen.

00:22:13.602 --> 00:22:16.806
Instead, she wakes up in the bathroom back at Annemarie's apartment where

00:22:16.828 --> 00:22:19.682
she finds the dog beheaded and the baby Anthony missing.

00:22:19.746 --> 00:22:22.182
Before long, police storm in and apprehend her.

00:22:22.236 --> 00:22:25.514
So why does Candyman frame her instead of just killing her?

00:22:25.552 --> 00:22:26.762
This will become more evident throughout his story.

00:22:26.816 --> 00:22:29.706
But I think Candyman sees something in Helen that reminds him of his love

00:22:29.728 --> 00:22:30.698
from 1890, Caroline.

00:22:30.784 --> 00:22:34.142
He doesn't just want to kill Helen, he wants her to join him.

00:22:34.196 --> 00:22:35.966
But we'll get into that a little bit later.

00:22:36.068 --> 00:22:39.086
After she's bailed out of jail he appears to her again by punching through

00:22:39.108 --> 00:22:40.510
her medicine cabinets.

00:22:42.850 --> 00:22:44.474
This method of entry is symbolic.

00:22:44.522 --> 00:22:47.522
It's the same way that Ruthie Jean's apartment was invaded four years ago.

00:22:47.576 --> 00:22:51.074
He's getting back at her for making people believe that Candyman isn't real.

00:22:51.192 --> 00:22:54.910
-[Candyman] Your disbelief destroy the faith of my congregation.

00:22:55.070 --> 00:22:58.546
It's interesting how he references his followers as his congregation,

00:22:58.568 --> 00:22:59.774
giving it a religious undertone.

00:22:59.822 --> 00:23:01.874
It's not the only religious parallel I noticed.

00:23:01.922 --> 00:23:04.514
His outfit almost makes it look as if he's wearing clergy robes.

00:23:04.562 --> 00:23:07.878
And non-diagetically, the gospel music that we hear when he's around makes it

00:23:07.884 --> 00:23:10.450
feel as if he's constantly being followed by a church choir.

00:23:10.530 --> 00:23:13.578
There will be more religious references, but I think the main takeaway is

00:23:13.584 --> 00:23:16.746
that he's being used to show the similarities between organized religion and

00:23:16.768 --> 00:23:17.798
urban legends.

00:23:17.894 --> 00:23:20.406
Both are supported by the belief of the congregation.

