old family tree.

00:47:02.958 --> 00:47:06.434
But when exactly does this happen? I had to get a little creative to figure

00:47:06.472 --> 00:47:07.010
it out.

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:10.486
So remember how I said weekdays in the Candyman world don't match up with

00:47:10.508 --> 00:47:13.894
weekdays in our world? Because on Anthony's computer, we see that the date

00:47:13.932 --> 00:47:15.922
is Tuesday, May 15th, 2019.

00:47:15.986 --> 00:47:18.130
But in our world, May 15th is on a Wednesday.

00:47:18.210 --> 00:47:21.570
So the day of the week in Candyman will always be one day earlier.

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:25.846
Well, in Candyman Day of the Dead, we hear this... 
-[XXX] Saturday's Day of the Dead.

00:47:25.878 --> 00:47:27.738
Day of the Dead begins on November 1.

00:47:27.824 --> 00:47:30.826
I made calendars with the adjusted weekdays of the Candyman universe and

00:47:30.848 --> 00:47:33.914
found four possible dates where Day of the Dead falls on Saturday in their world.

00:47:33.952 --> 00:47:39.390
November 2015, November 2020, November 2026, and November 2037.

00:47:39.460 --> 00:47:43.374
Now, if you'll recall, Caroline was born in 1995 at the end of

00:47:43.412 --> 00:47:44.490
Candyman: Farewell to the Flesh

00:47:44.570 --> 00:47:46.734
So in 2015, she would be 20 years old.

00:47:46.772 --> 00:47:48.894
In 2020, she would be 25 years old.

00:47:48.932 --> 00:47:53.870
In 2026, she would be 31 years old, and in 2037, she would be 42 years old.

00:47:53.940 --> 00:47:57.206
The actress who plays Caroline Line in Candyman: Day of the Dead was

00:47:57.228 --> 00:48:00.246
31 years old at the time it came out, so it would appear that it takes place in 2026.

00:48:00.268 --> 00:48:04.182
I have noticed a couple of websites claiming that it's 25 years after

00:48:04.236 --> 00:48:05.234
Farewell to the Flesh

00:48:05.282 --> 00:48:07.846
However, I am going to stick with the evidence that is actually in the movies.

00:48:07.868 --> 00:48:09.046
ANYWAY.

00:48:09.148 --> 00:48:12.198
Caroline's friend Miguel is holding an art gallery in LA to show off the

00:48:12.204 --> 00:48:14.662
works of Daniel Robitaille, which Caroline has inherited.

00:48:14.726 --> 00:48:18.106
She wants people to appreciate Daniel Robitaille the artist, not exploit him

00:48:18.128 --> 00:48:19.990
for being at the center of the Candyman myth.

00:48:20.070 --> 00:48:22.682
Miguel understands marketing a little better than she does, though.

00:48:22.736 --> 00:48:26.298
For example, I easily could have marketed this video as what Daniel Robitaille

00:48:26.314 --> 00:48:29.726
taught us about urban legends and interracial relations, a video essay, and

00:48:29.748 --> 00:48:32.550
gotten like 15 views, but I didn't do that, thankfully.

00:48:32.650 --> 00:48:35.906
But Miguel's promotional genius ends up bringing in a little bit more of an

00:48:35.928 --> 00:48:39.140
unsavory crowd, and things get a little out of hand.

00:48:39.140 --> 00:48:41.696
(IMPACT)

00:48:44.129 --> 00:48:47.418
♪ Mysterious Music ♪

00:48:47.990 --> 00:48:51.094
As Miguel tells his audience about Robitaille's origin story.

00:48:51.132 --> 00:48:54.854
We see this scene of him tied to a tree where his body is ravaged by bees at night,

00:48:54.892 --> 00:48:58.198
whereas before it appeared to be during the day and he was on the ground, 

00:48:58.204 --> 00:49:01.478
you could look at this as a continuity error, but again, I choose to

00:49:01.484 --> 00:49:03.942
see it as an example of the nature of urban legends.

00:49:04.006 --> 00:49:06.182
It's like the telephone game I mentioned previously.

00:49:06.246 --> 00:49:09.270
Details get changed as it's passed from generation to generation.

00:49:09.350 --> 00:49:12.458
I choose to see the depiction featured in Candyman 2: Farewell to the Flesh

00:49:12.464 --> 00:49:15.434
as what actually happened, because Annie seems to receive this vision

00:49:15.482 --> 00:49:19.198
directly from Candyman, whereas the depiction in Candyman 3: Day of the Dead

00:49:19.204 --> 00:49:22.286
is shown while an art dealer is attempting to tell Candyman's story, or

00:49:22.308 --> 00:49:24.190
at least the version of the story that he's heard.

00:49:24.260 --> 00:49:26.910
What we see here is just a visual representation of that.

00:49:26.980 --> 00:49:29.826
At one point during the evening, Miguel dares Caroline to prove that she

00:49:29.848 --> 00:49:32.254
doesn't believe in Candyman by calling his name into the mirror.

00:49:32.302 --> 00:49:35.618
She pauses a modest 3 seconds before completing the ritual, but she and the

00:49:35.624 --> 00:49:38.642
other guests are terrified when the mirror breaks and a hook handed man

00:49:38.696 --> 00:49:39.790
storms into the gallery.

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:42.806
This turned out to only be an actor that Miguel had hired for the event, but

00:49:42.828 --> 00:49:46.514
the curse that she brought upon herself was real, but nothing supernatural

00:49:46.562 --> 00:49:48.006
would happen until that night.

00:49:48.108 --> 00:49:50.822
Also, I'm not really sure what Miguel was trying to do here.

00:49:50.876 --> 00:49:54.134
It doesn't seem like having a raving lunatic apparently crash your gallery

00:49:54.182 --> 00:49:55.718
would help you sell more artwork.

00:49:55.814 --> 00:49:58.570
Maybe he's not the marketing genius that I made him out to be.

00:49:58.640 --> 00:50:01.918
That evening on the subway, Candyman's voice echoes through the tunnels and

00:50:01.924 --> 00:50:06.234
she sees a swarm of bees approach as he floats in her direction and gives her the usual spiel.

00:50:06.234 --> 00:50:09.536
-[Candyman] Bee-lieve... in me. 

00:50:09.536 --> 00:50:12.770
Bee-lieve? Please don’t tell me that’s another pun, please.

00:50:13.330 --> 00:50:17.082
Anyway, Caroline passes out, but the activity for that evening continues.

00:50:17.146 --> 00:50:20.718
He goes after Miguel and his date, a model who is literally smearing honey

00:50:20.734 --> 00:50:22.142
on herself with a honeycomb.

00:50:22.206 --> 00:50:24.954
You're just begging Candyman to kill you at that point, which he does.

00:50:24.954 --> 00:50:25.896
Of course.

00:50:25.896 --> 00:50:29.426
Miguel gets the old hook and the model is stung by bees, and it appears she

00:50:29.448 --> 00:50:31.382
might have gotten the sharp end of the hook as well.

00:50:31.436 --> 00:50:32.902
But I obviously can't show that.

00:50:32.956 --> 00:50:35.238
But I will show the message that he left behind on the wall... Believe.

00:50:35.324 --> 00:50:38.502
This has been Candyman's main goal for a while now.

00:50:38.556 --> 00:50:41.926
It's been 136 years since this legend began, and despite there being

00:50:41.948 --> 00:50:45.046
evidence of his existence, it seems like nobody really believes in him or

00:50:45.068 --> 00:50:45.842
fears him anymore.

00:50:45.906 --> 00:50:48.930
It actually reminded me a lot of the episode that I did on Freddy Krueger.

00:50:49.010 --> 00:50:52.446
As the years go by, people stop being afraid and these entities need people

00:50:52.468 --> 00:50:54.474
to believe in them in order to maintain their powers.

00:50:54.522 --> 00:50:56.734
We even saw something similar with Pennywise in

00:50:56.772 --> 00:50:57.390
IT: Chapter Two.

00:50:57.460 --> 00:51:00.394
I guess I'm finding that this is a pretty common trope in the horror genre.

00:51:00.442 --> 00:51:04.046
And honestly, this idea of belief giving an entity power could be rooted in reality.

00:51:04.068 --> 00:51:07.306
If you recall, at the beginning of this video, I discussed Bloody Mary

00:51:07.338 --> 00:51:10.098
and how some people who performed the ritual actually believed that

00:51:10.104 --> 00:51:12.754
they did see her in the mirror because they believed that they would see her.

00:51:12.792 --> 00:51:16.622
In a sense, she's literally only appearing to those who believe belief

00:51:16.686 --> 00:51:17.586
is her power.

00:51:17.688 --> 00:51:21.586
Caroline eventually discovers Miguel, and a duo of comically racist cops

00:51:21.618 --> 00:51:22.310
gets on the case.

00:51:22.380 --> 00:51:23.490
Here's a highlight reel.

00:51:23.570 --> 00:51:25.430
-[Kraft] Your kind's always up to something.

00:51:25.580 --> 00:51:27.954
-[Kraft] Get back to your business, Miguel.

00:51:28.082 --> 00:51:31.807
-[Kraft] Then he goes and gets himself killed. Ay caramba!

00:51:32.010 --> 00:51:37.082
-[Kraft] Like you said, Jamal, why don't you go shoot some hoops.

00:51:37.136 --> 00:51:40.038
Long story short, the paintings from the art gallery were all stolen on the night of

00:51:40.054 --> 00:51:42.698
Miguel's death, and his friend David, the actor who he had hired to crash the

00:51:42.704 --> 00:51:44.182
gallery, is a possible suspect.

00:51:44.246 --> 00:51:47.614
So he and Caroline team up to retrieve the paintings and clear David's name.

00:51:47.652 --> 00:51:50.734
But Caroline starts seeing more and more evidence of her great great great

00:51:50.772 --> 00:51:53.482
grandpa Robitaille being more than just a doubted myth.

00:51:53.546 --> 00:51:56.402
She steps into the bathroom while David is asking around for info at a bar

00:51:56.456 --> 00:51:59.406
where Candyman appears and shows her imagery of her dead mother.

00:51:59.518 --> 00:52:01.714
-[Candyman] Our story shall be one.

00:52:01.912 --> 00:52:07.326
-[Candyman] One destiny, one family, one blood.

00:52:07.438 --> 00:52:12.467
And I can't decide if that comes off more like a Bob Marley lyric or more like a religious passage.

00:52:12.467 --> 00:52:14.109
-[Candyman] ♪ One blood… ♪

00:52:14.570 --> 00:52:17.878
Yeah, now that I think about it, one love does have religious lyrics, and

00:52:17.884 --> 00:52:20.834
it's just my own fault that I associate it more with a tropical beach.

00:52:20.882 --> 00:52:23.766
But just the way that Candyman talks uses a lot of words that you might

00:52:23.788 --> 00:52:25.398
expect to find in a religious text.

00:52:25.484 --> 00:52:29.094
The word blood can just as easily refer to violence or sacrifice as it can

00:52:29.132 --> 00:52:30.746
to family connections and bloodlines.

00:52:30.778 --> 00:52:33.806
Caroline is supposedly the last survivor of his bloodline, and he's trying

00:52:33.828 --> 00:52:36.074
to get her to join with the rest of their family in death.

00:52:36.122 --> 00:52:38.046
So in this case, it kinda refers to both.

00:52:38.148 --> 00:52:41.854
This time, Caroline freaks out, having accepted the existence of the family

00:52:41.892 --> 00:52:43.434
phantom of fudgy and fruity snacks.

00:52:43.482 --> 00:52:47.066
So David takes her to see his grandmother, a curandera or a healer of evil

00:52:47.098 --> 00:52:48.842
spirits in some Latin American cultures.

00:52:48.906 --> 00:52:51.422
She tells him that evil cannot exist without good.

00:52:51.476 --> 00:52:54.950
But her visions of Candyman are getting worse as she sees a bee crawl out

00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:56.674
from a bloody red spot in the egg yolk.

00:52:56.722 --> 00:53:00.118
In this ritual, the egg is basically supposed to sweep up any bad mojo that

00:53:00.124 --> 00:53:01.062
might be attached to her.

00:53:01.116 --> 00:53:04.038
In another video, I think I described it as the spiritual equivalent of a

00:53:04.044 --> 00:53:07.202
paper towel picking up dirt off a surface in order to cleanse the subject.

00:53:07.266 --> 00:53:10.054
I think it's probably the first time we've seen anyone do anything to try to

00:53:10.092 --> 00:53:12.914
detach themselves from Candyman after saying his name in the mirror.

00:53:12.962 --> 00:53:14.506
It's a good idea, but it doesn't work.

00:53:14.528 --> 00:53:17.706
And Caroline asks David to stay with her until her roommate gets home so she

00:53:17.728 --> 00:53:18.758
doesn't have to be alone.

00:53:18.854 --> 00:53:21.690
This of course leads to them going to bed together.

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:25.990
But at some point during this encounter, David is replaced with Daniel.

00:53:26.070 --> 00:53:27.746
That is Daniel Robitaille.

00:53:27.798 --> 00:53:31.546
It was one thing when he was trying to kiss Helen Lyle in 1992, but dude,

00:53:31.578 --> 00:53:33.562
this is your great great great granddaughter.

00:53:33.626 --> 00:53:34.890
Like, that's kind of weird.

00:53:34.970 --> 00:53:36.254
I don't really like it.

00:53:36.292 --> 00:53:39.694
I think the significance is supposed to be that there's an intimacy subtext

00:53:39.742 --> 00:53:43.218
because Caroline McKeever is named after Caroline Sullivan, his lover from

00:53:43.224 --> 00:53:43.934
when he was alive.

00:53:43.982 --> 00:53:47.506
But this is just a little less Louisiana and a little more Alabama,

00:53:47.528 --> 00:53:48.270
if you catch my drift.

00:53:48.350 --> 00:53:51.414
He tells her that he'll let David live only if she takes his place and

00:53:51.452 --> 00:53:52.370
becomes his victim.

00:53:52.450 --> 00:53:55.318
Basically the same offer that he made to Helen Lyle, allowing her to be the

00:53:55.324 --> 00:53:56.754
victim instead of the baby Anthony.

00:53:56.802 --> 00:53:58.114
But this time it's reversed.

00:53:58.162 --> 00:54:00.854
David has a young daughter who would be orphaned if something were to happen

00:54:00.892 --> 00:54:01.392
to him.

00:54:01.420 --> 00:54:04.858
Once again, Caroline passes out and her roommate eventually finds her in the

00:54:04.864 --> 00:54:05.734
midst of a nightmare.

00:54:05.782 --> 00:54:08.854
When she wakes up, she's spouting off about how Candyman has taken David.

00:54:08.902 --> 00:54:11.594
But from Tamara's point of view, it looks like she's lost it.

00:54:11.712 --> 00:54:12.746
-[Caroline] I'm not crazy.

00:54:12.848 --> 00:54:13.926
-[Tamara] I didn't say you were crazy.

00:54:13.968 --> 00:54:15.422
-[Tamara] But you need to talk to somebody.

00:54:15.556 --> 00:54:16.766
-[Tamara] You just need some help.

00:54:16.868 --> 00:54:18.542
Yeah, I didn't say you were crazy.

00:54:18.676 --> 00:54:22.046
You just need to talk to a professional because you're crazy.

00:54:22.228 --> 00:54:25.962
Candyman does have a way with making his victims look mentally unwell.

00:54:26.026 --> 00:54:29.246
He did the same thing to Helen who got locked up in an asylum because of it.

00:54:29.268 --> 00:54:32.158
And to a lesser extent, he made Anthony McCoy look like he'd gone off the

00:54:32.164 --> 00:54:32.990
deep end too.

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:36.178
You may have noticed at this point that this feels contradictory.

00:54:36.274 --> 00:54:39.878
If he wants people to believe in him, then why make his victims look crazy?

00:54:39.964 --> 00:54:41.522
It's working against his main goal.

00:54:41.586 --> 00:54:44.438
Would you be more likely to believe a ghost story from a crazy person or a sane person?

00:54:44.444 --> 00:54:47.926
This is probably the single biggest struggle that I had while

00:54:47.948 --> 00:54:49.602
crafting my analysis of this character.

00:54:49.666 --> 00:54:52.498
His actions don't seem to match up with his stated intention.

00:54:52.594 --> 00:54:55.274
At the end of the day, we don't have any explanation for this.

00:54:55.312 --> 00:54:58.426
I'm just left to assume that there's some kind of rule preventing him from

00:54:58.448 --> 00:55:00.762
providing hard evidence to get people to believe in him.

00:55:00.816 --> 00:55:03.754
Or maybe he only benefits from their belief in him if they don't actually

00:55:03.792 --> 00:55:05.414
have proof that he exists.

00:55:05.542 --> 00:55:08.718
One thing I kept coming back to is the security tape of him taking out the

00:55:08.724 --> 00:55:10.478
police officer in 1995.

00:55:10.564 --> 00:55:13.522
Shouldn't this have gotten a whole bunch of people to believe in him?

00:55:13.576 --> 00:55:17.794
Well, after going over all of my Candyman notes, I think I may have

00:55:17.832 --> 00:55:18.420
found the answer.

00:55:18.420 --> 00:55:21.045
(IMPACT)

00:55:23.101 --> 00:55:27.095
♪ Synth Music ♪

00:55:27.270 --> 00:55:30.818
One theme that has come up over and over throughout Candyman's sordid

00:55:30.834 --> 00:55:34.134
history is the comparison of how people see him as an urban legend and how

00:55:34.172 --> 00:55:36.178
devotees interact with religion.

00:55:36.274 --> 00:55:39.794
From his clothing to his sermon like monologues to the many other references

00:55:39.842 --> 00:55:42.918
and religious subtext that I've pointed out in this video, I think the lack

00:55:42.934 --> 00:55:46.266
of definitive evidence provided to make people believe in him operates the

00:55:46.288 --> 00:55:47.542
same way as religion.

00:55:47.606 --> 00:55:49.350
Religion is all about faith.

00:55:49.430 --> 00:55:52.778
Followers of a religion may have a relationship with God, but it's never in

00:55:52.784 --> 00:55:56.126
the form of irrefutable proof that God exists, because that wouldn't be faith.

00:55:56.148 --> 00:55:58.362
That would just be like proof.

00:55:58.426 --> 00:56:01.962
It reminds me of this old meme pointing out how God is always intervening,

00:56:02.026 --> 00:56:05.562
talking to prophets, creating miracles, and delivering messages to mankind.

00:56:05.626 --> 00:56:08.706
But then after the invention of the video camera, he just never shows up again.

00:56:08.728 --> 00:56:11.374
Obviously it's just a meme, it's just a joke.

00:56:11.422 --> 00:56:14.594
I think you can believe in God even if you don't see the biblical stories as

00:56:14.632 --> 00:56:15.810
literal events that happened.

00:56:15.880 --> 00:56:19.030
But the main point that I'm getting at here is that those who believe do so

00:56:19.100 --> 00:56:20.082
even without proof.

00:56:20.146 --> 00:56:23.506
And the point behind Candyman is that urban legends and supernatural hook

00:56:23.538 --> 00:56:25.750
handed killers operate very much the same way.

00:56:25.820 --> 00:56:28.726
The mystery around the whole thing creates believers, whether it be

00:56:28.748 --> 00:56:32.274
Candyman, Bloody Mary, the Hookman, or any other urban legend.

00:56:32.322 --> 00:56:34.710
And this is what gives power to those legends.

00:56:34.790 --> 00:56:38.218
And this would also kind of explain why Candyman likes to have one victim in

00:56:38.224 --> 00:56:39.574
each movie that he keeps alive for most of it.

00:56:39.622 --> 00:56:42.986
He wants Caroline to believe in him and fear him, and it is

00:56:43.008 --> 00:56:45.182
her faith that will inspire others to do the same.

00:56:45.236 --> 00:56:47.882
And this is pretty much how it goes in every installment of Candyman.

00:56:47.946 --> 00:56:51.262
So Caroline's situation goes very much like Helen's did in 92.

00:56:51.316 --> 00:56:53.722
He kills her roommate, just as he killed Bernadette.

00:56:53.786 --> 00:56:56.442
This gets Caroline arrested just as Helen was arrested.

00:56:56.506 --> 00:56:59.506
Then he kills the police officer holding her captive, just as he killed the

00:56:59.528 --> 00:57:01.182
psychiatrist who kept Helen captive.

00:57:01.246 --> 00:57:04.718
Caroline is giving the officer's keys so she can escape, just as Helen's

00:57:04.734 --> 00:57:08.002
restraints were cut loose so that she could escape.

00:57:08.056 --> 00:57:10.806
With nowhere else to go, Caroline goes to David's house and gets another

00:57:10.828 --> 00:57:13.122
blessing from his grandmother, who elaborates on the advice from earlier.

00:57:13.186 --> 00:57:17.414
She says evil cannot exist without good and she must destroy good in order

00:57:17.452 --> 00:57:18.498
to destroy evil.

00:57:18.594 --> 00:57:20.594
This doesn't seem like very good advice.

00:57:20.642 --> 00:57:23.578
And Caroline interprets it to mean that she has to destroy the paintings of

00:57:23.584 --> 00:57:24.662
Caroline Sullivan.

00:57:24.726 --> 00:57:27.814
So she evades the police by blending through the Day of the Dead celebration

00:57:27.862 --> 00:57:28.934
taking place in the streets.

00:57:28.982 --> 00:57:32.106
And Candyman tries to convince her to give herself up to him along the way.

00:57:32.128 --> 00:57:35.606
To her finally locating the gangsters who stole Daniel Robitaille's artwork.

00:57:35.718 --> 00:57:38.926
This new metal basis looking guy claims that the paintings are haunted and

00:57:38.948 --> 00:57:40.110
he wants her to take them away.

00:57:40.180 --> 00:57:41.770
But oh, no, it's a trap.

00:57:41.850 --> 00:57:44.074
She gets knocked out by a goth dude with a hook.

00:57:44.122 --> 00:57:48.162
Caroline's got it pretty rough, okay? She gets knocked out like every 20 minutes.

00:57:48.216 --> 00:57:51.874
She awakes to what appears to be a Marilyn Manson cover band, but

00:57:51.912 --> 00:57:54.722
soon finds out that it's actually not that at all.

00:57:54.776 --> 00:57:57.998
This is essentially Candyman's cult gathered to perform a ritual to honor

00:57:58.014 --> 00:57:59.070
him for the Day of the Dead.

00:57:59.150 --> 00:58:02.646
This is the result of the religious figure aura that he's given off for his

00:58:02.668 --> 00:58:03.554
entire afterlife.

00:58:03.602 --> 00:58:07.142
He now has followers performing ceremonies in his honor, even if they are

00:58:07.196 --> 00:58:09.110
trying way too hard to sound edgy.

00:58:09.190 --> 00:58:12.262
-[Cult Member] We had an epiphany at the gallery.

00:58:12.406 --> 00:58:19.322
-[Cult Member] His myth called to us a reflection of the evil that created you.

00:58:19.456 --> 00:58:23.310
Make sure you subscribe for new horrors every week.

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:26.640
Otherwise I'll feel black and empty inside.

00:58:27.250 --> 00:58:30.878
One of these Antichrist superstars gets the honor of summoning him in a

00:58:30.884 --> 00:58:34.174
broken mirror, taking a five second dramatic pause at the end.

00:58:34.212 --> 00:58:37.490
When he shows up, he does a lot of damage, ripping out the piercings of the leader

00:58:37.560 --> 00:58:41.106
before slashing his neck and presumably doing the same to all or most of the others.

00:58:41.128 --> 00:58:45.186
He makes one last offer to Caroline, proposing to let David live in

00:58:45.208 --> 00:58:48.146
exchange for her life and overwhelmed by his presence.

00:58:48.258 --> 00:58:52.614
I guess even though she's seen him six times before this, she passes out again.

00:58:52.732 --> 00:58:55.560
Maybe it's time to see someone about that.

00:58:55.560 --> 00:58:57.560
-[Tamara] You need to talk to somebody. You just need some help.

00:58:58.170 --> 00:58:59.206
And she wakes up.

00:58:59.228 --> 00:59:02.486
She finds a hook and discovers the paintings of Candyman, seemingly guarded

00:59:02.518 --> 00:59:03.702
by thousands of beads.

00:59:03.766 --> 00:59:06.986
But before she can find the courage to approach, she's distracted when she

00:59:07.008 --> 00:59:10.454
spots David, still alive but hanging on a pair of industrial hooks.

00:59:10.502 --> 00:59:11.622
Bobby Dagen style.

00:59:11.686 --> 00:59:14.666
Since he is still alive, I'll assume that there's no issue with me showing

00:59:14.698 --> 00:59:15.338
this footage.

00:59:15.434 --> 00:59:19.198
But as she tries to save him, Candyman appears one last time and kind of

00:59:19.204 --> 00:59:20.426
confirms what I was theorizing about earlier.

00:59:20.458 --> 00:59:23.954
About him seeing Caroline as a metaphorical stand in for his Caroline.

00:59:23.992 --> 00:59:31.650
-[Candyman] At last, I will have what was taken from me so many, many years ago.

00:59:31.720 --> 00:59:37.998
-[Candyman] My dignity, my destiny, my blood.

00:59:38.174 --> 00:59:39.810
And he kisses her.

00:59:39.880 --> 00:59:41.798
Or maybe she kisses him.

00:59:41.884 --> 00:59:44.758
Just goes to show that some things are better not kept in the family.

00:59:44.844 --> 00:59:47.574
But it is actually Caroline's family that pulls her out of this.

00:59:47.612 --> 00:59:50.754
She has a vision of her dead mother causing her to change her tune, climb

00:59:50.802 --> 00:59:53.498
through the swarm of bees, and destroy the paintings of Robitaille and his

00:59:53.504 --> 00:59:57.526
girlfriend with, ironically, a hook, the tool that Candyman used to rip

00:59:57.558 --> 00:59:59.878
apart so many of his countless victims.

00:59:59.974 --> 01:00:03.386
She also knocks over some candles, setting the whole display on fire, just

01:00:03.408 --> 01:00:05.534
as Candyman was set ablaze all of those years.

01:00:10.668 --> 01:00:13.965
-[Candyman] AHHHHH!!!!!!!!!!!

01:00:13.965 --> 01:00:18.461
And Caroline feels that she has finally ended the 136 year legacy of the

01:00:18.483 --> 01:00:19.261
Candyman.

01:00:19.363 --> 01:00:21.593
-[Caroline] They all blamed someone else for your crimes.

01:00:21.689 --> 01:00:22.685
-[Caroline] I destroyed you.

01:00:22.755 --> 01:00:24.153
-[Caroline] You're good, and you're evil.

01:00:24.249 --> 01:00:25.373
-[Caroline] There's nothing left.

01:00:25.459 --> 01:00:27.401
-[Caroline] There's no proof of your existence.

01:00:27.513 --> 01:00:28.697
-[Caroline] I destroyed the myth.

01:00:28.761 --> 01:00:30.513
-[Caroline] No one will ever call your name again.

01:00:30.599 --> 01:00:33.889
Which, honestly, has got to be the most stupid and naive thing ever, because

01:00:33.927 --> 01:00:37.501
there is literally graffiti all over the place in multiple cities depicting

01:00:37.533 --> 01:00:40.829
the Candyman, not to mention the book that was written by Purcell, countless

01:00:40.877 --> 01:00:44.269
news articles, but most importantly, multiple generations telling the tale

01:00:44.317 --> 01:00:45.453
via oral tradition.

01:00:45.549 --> 01:00:48.429
There's no way you could possibly wipe out a myth like Candyman.

01:00:48.477 --> 01:00:51.369
And even attempting to do so would most likely to have a Streisand effect

01:00:51.407 --> 01:00:52.937
and bring even more attention to him.

01:00:52.991 --> 01:00:55.913
Police show up on the scene shortly after, and you know that this means that

01:00:55.919 --> 01:00:58.901
there'll be reports filed, and this will end up on the Internet and just fan

01:00:58.933 --> 01:01:00.357
the fire of Candyman rumors.

01:01:00.421 --> 01:01:03.561
It seems like if you truly wanted to neutralize Candyman, the best way to go

01:01:03.583 --> 01:01:05.365
about it would be through narrative drift.

01:01:05.445 --> 01:01:08.409
For example, if you intentionally spread a bunch of rumors saying you only

01:01:08.447 --> 01:01:10.969
have to say the name three times, then that'll probably end up getting

01:01:11.007 --> 01:01:13.657
spread around, and a decent amount of people will believe it.

01:01:13.711 --> 01:01:16.881
I mean, saying the name three times works for Bloody Mary, Beetlejuice, and

01:01:16.903 --> 01:01:18.717
Biggie Smalls, so it seems kind of believable.

01:01:18.781 --> 01:01:21.681
That reminds me of this Quora post I saw one time that said, what would

01:01:21.703 --> 01:01:24.833
happen if you said Candyman, Beetlejuice, Bloody Mary, three times in a mirror.

01:01:24.839 --> 01:01:28.161
And the person who answered it was just like, absolutely nothing.

01:01:28.263 --> 01:01:30.013
None of these beings even exist.

01:01:30.109 --> 01:01:32.353
Like, not even entertaining the fun part of the question.

01:01:32.439 --> 01:01:35.249
But, yeah, your best bet would just be to intentionally change the legend

01:01:35.297 --> 01:01:38.629
over time so that it no longer resembles the original story or just not

01:01:38.667 --> 01:01:40.597
acknowledge it like what Ethan Tarrant tried to do.

01:01:40.651 --> 01:01:43.541
But at the end of the day, it takes more than one person to do either of

01:01:43.563 --> 01:01:46.569
those things, which means the legend will probably always persist in some form.

01:01:46.607 --> 01:01:49.893
So does that mean that we haven't actually seen the last of the

01:01:49.909 --> 01:01:50.745
Candyman? Maybe.

01:01:50.815 --> 01:01:52.905
But personally, I think his story has been told.

01:01:52.975 --> 01:01:55.401
There's not a whole lot that could really be added to it, and it's not

01:01:55.423 --> 01:01:58.489
really as relevant in a cultural sense as it was when Candyman was first created.

01:01:58.527 --> 01:02:01.961
The original Candyman highlighted how the racial segregation of the

01:02:01.983 --> 01:02:04.009
1890s was still alive in a different form in the 1990s.

01:02:04.047 --> 01:02:07.789
Farewell to the Flesh brought back the idea, putting a spotlight on Octavia,

01:02:07.837 --> 01:02:11.437
 who sought to erase any racial diversity in her family tree.

01:02:11.501 --> 01:02:13.613
Again, a pretty subtle theme about segregation.

01:02:13.709 --> 01:02:17.521
But now, as of recording in the 2020s, we're not that far removed from

01:02:17.543 --> 01:02:19.745
George Floyd being the biggest story in the world.

01:02:19.815 --> 01:02:22.497
And we already got a Candyman movie on the heels of that.

01:02:22.551 --> 01:02:25.857
It seems kind of like revisiting it again is just beating a dead horse.

01:02:25.921 --> 01:02:27.909
It's not like there still aren't bad people in the world.

01:02:27.947 --> 01:02:31.253
But the original Candyman was much more of a nuanced commentary, putting a

01:02:31.259 --> 01:02:33.809
spotlight on how segregation is still hiding in plain sight.

01:02:33.857 --> 01:02:37.589
And now it's kind of devolved into very on the nose references to literally

01:02:37.637 --> 01:02:39.433
some of the biggest news stories in the world.

01:02:39.519 --> 01:02:41.957
Candyman showed us how a new legend is born.

01:02:42.021 --> 01:02:45.113
But what started as a series about the nature of urban legends and the

01:02:45.119 --> 01:02:48.217
intersection of myth and reality has seemingly become more focused on

01:02:48.271 --> 01:02:49.973
reminding us about reality.

01:02:50.069 --> 01:02:54.217
Unlike Candyman's many victims, I'm not so sure we need to repeat his name

01:02:54.271 --> 01:02:55.137
for a fifth time.

01:02:55.231 --> 01:02:58.481
But to get the full picture on Urban Legends, what they have to say and how

01:02:58.503 --> 01:02:59.981
they've been interpreted in horror movies.

01:03:00.013 --> 01:03:03.117
I've got a playlist lined up for you on left with some other great examples.

01:03:03.181 --> 01:03:06.177
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every week.

01:03:06.231 --> 01:03:10.273
Ring the deathbell for all notifications and I will see you in the next one.

01:03:10.359 --> 01:03:12.285
Assuming we both survive.

